<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    
    <!-- NuGet Package Metadata -->
    <Title>EffinitiveFramework.Core</Title>
    <Authors>Henry Bartosch</Authors>
    <PackageId>EffinitiveFramework.Core</PackageId>
    <Version>2.6.0</Version>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/HBartosch/Effinitive</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/HBartosch/Effinitive</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PackageTags>http;http2;web-framework;high-performance;zero-allocation;minimal-api;aspnetcore-alternative;hpack;alpn;tls;https;fast;low-latency;microservices;sse;server-sent-events;streaming</PackageTags>
    <Description>Ultra-high-performance HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 web framework for .NET 8+. Built from scratch for maximum speed with zero-allocation routing, custom HTTP/2 implementation with HPACK compression, and sub-50μs response times. 6-10x faster than ASP.NET Core for API workloads.</Description>
    <PackageIcon>PackageIconResized.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE</PackageLicenseFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>v2.6.0: Multiple listeners, cleartext HTTP/2, and certificate rotation without a restart. AddListener() adds a socket beyond UsePort() and UseHttpsPort(), each with its own certificate and its own ALPN list, so one server can advertise HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 on one port while advertising HTTP/1.1 alone on another. Per RFC 7301 3.2 the server selects from what the client advertised and its own preference governs, so that list decides what a listener serves. UseHttp2Cleartext serves HTTP/2 with prior knowledge (RFC 9113 3.3), validating the 3.4 connection preface as the TLS path does, so a client speaking HTTP/1.1 on such a port is rejected rather than quietly served. TlsOptions.ReloadOnChange resolves the certificate per handshake through ServerCertificateSelectionCallback, so a renewal on disk takes effect on the next handshake while established connections keep the certificate they negotiated with; the files are polled rather than watched, because renewal tools rename a new pair over the old one and inotify reports that unreliably across container filesystems. Fixes: a header line whose CRLF was split across two TCP reads was answered 400 rather than carried, though RFC 9110 15.5.1 reserves 400 for a client error and where a read ended is not one; a WebSocket reply was held back by a partly-arrived following frame, because the write-batching test asked whether bytes remained rather than whether a whole frame had, and RFC 6455 5.2 frames carry an explicit length; and TLS connections now send a close_notify alert before closing, which RFC 8446 6.1 requires and SslStream.DisposeAsync does not do, without which a truncated response is indistinguishable from a complete one.

v2.5.0: Rate limiting. UseRateLimiting() applies a token-bucket allowance per client IP across HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, returning 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After and a ProblemDetails body. Applied before routing, so static files, the OpenAPI document, and floods at nonexistent paths are covered. [RateLimit(PermitLimit, WindowSeconds)] gives an endpoint its own tighter allowance and [DisableRateLimit] exempts one entirely. X-Forwarded-For is honored only from configured trusted proxies, walking the chain right-to-left. Tracked clients are capped so rotating source addresses cannot exhaust memory. Also adds HttpRequest.RemoteIpAddress and 429 to the cached status lines.

v2.4.0: OpenAPI/Swagger integration. UseOpenApi() serves an OpenAPI 3.0.3 document at /openapi/v1.json and Swagger UI at /swagger, generated once at startup from the route table and served from a pre-built buffer, so there is no per-request cost. Routes, path parameter types, request bodies, response schemas, and content types are inferred from existing metadata; [OpenApiOperation], [OpenApiResponse], [OpenApiParameter], and [OpenApiIgnore] add what cannot be. Schema property names follow the server's JsonSerializerOptions and DataAnnotations become schema constraints. Security schemes match the built-in JWT and API-key handlers. Also adds Router.GetRegisteredRoutes() and EndpointInvoker.ResponseType.

v2.3.0: Response caching. UseResponseCaching() serves repeat GET/HEAD requests from a bounded in-process store, skipping endpoint execution and JSON serialization, and emits Cache-Control/Vary/Age for client and proxy caches. Opt-in per endpoint via [ResponseCache(Duration, Location, VaryByHeader, VaryByQueryKeys)]. Entries expire on their duration and are evicted when a successful POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE hits the same path. Cache hits answer If-None-Match with 304 on HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3. Requests carrying Authorization bypass the shared cache unless the endpoint opts in. Also fixes HTTP/2 outbound flow control: the send path now honors the peer's stream- and connection-level send windows (RFC 7540 6.9) instead of queueing whole bodies at once, and the connection send window starts at the RFC default of 65535 with the client's SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE tracked separately.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2025 Henry Bartosch</Copyright>
    <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>false</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
    <GeneratePackageOnBuild>false</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="EffinitiveFramework.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- HTTP/3 (QUIC) only available on .NET 10+ -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
    <Compile Remove="Http3\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.NetAnalyzers" Version="9.0.0" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.NetAnalyzers" Version="10.0.100" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.ObjectPool" Version="8.0.0" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.ObjectPool" Version="10.0.0" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'" />
    <PackageReference Include="SecurityCodeScan.VS2019" Version="5.6.7">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Pipelines" Version="8.0.0" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Pipelines" Version="10.0.0" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt" Version="8.16.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Routya.ResultKit" Version="2.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\PackageIconResized.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
